<main id="content">

  <article>
    <header class="art-head" data-od-id="article-head">
      <div class="shell">
        <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol>
            <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
            <li><a href="/recording/">Recording</a></li>
            <li>Strings</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <h1>How to Record Viola at Home</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A viola in a small room is mostly a room problem. Placement, the treatment
          you can actually do in a rented flat, and the three microphone positions
          worth trying before you change anything else.
        </p>
        <dl class="byline">
          <div><dt>Written by</dt><dd class="byline__v">Auspicious Music editorial</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Published</dt><dd class="byline__v"><time datetime="2026-07-11">11 July 2026</time></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Reading time</dt><dd class="byline__v">16 min</dd></div>
        </dl>

        <section class="requires measure">
          <h2>What this assumes</h2>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Instrument</dt><dd>Viola — the same method applies to violin at a shorter distance</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Microphones</dt><dd>One large-diaphragm condenser, or a small-diaphragm pair</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Interface</dt><dd>Any interface with clean gain, 48 kHz / 24-bit</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Room</dt><dd>An ordinary domestic room, untreated or lightly treated</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Measured data</dt><dd>Outstanding — A/B recordings to follow</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="shell band band--tight">
      <div class="art-grid">
        <details class="toc" open>
          <summary>Contents</summary>
          <nav aria-label="Table of contents">
            <ol>
              <li><a href="#room">Start with the room, not the microphone</a></li>
              <li><a href="#where">Where the instrument actually radiates</a></li>
              <li><a href="#positions">Three positions worth trying</a></li>
              <li><a href="#gain">Gain and headroom</a></li>
              <li><a href="#player">What the player needs</a></li>
              <li><a href="#after">What to fix afterwards, and what not to</a></li>
            </ol>
          </nav>
        </details>

        <div class="prose" data-od-id="article-body">

          <h2 id="room" style="margin-top:0">Start with the room, not the microphone</h2>
          <p>
            Almost every disappointing string recording made at home is a room
            recording with an instrument in it. A viola projects in every direction and
            keeps sounding for as long as the room lets it; a microphone a metre away
            picks up far more wall than most people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before changing a microphone, change where you stand. Playing towards the
            middle of a room rather than into a corner removes more problems than any
            preamp will. Where a wall cannot be avoided, something soft on it at ear
            height — a duvet on a clothes rail is not a joke — will do more than a
            single foam tile.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Standing waves are a matter of dimensions, not decoration.</strong>
              A room reinforces frequencies whose half-wavelength matches one of its
              dimensions. The
              <a href="/resources/tools/note-frequency/">note and frequency tool</a> lists
              wavelength beside every pitch — if a particular note booms in one spot and
              vanishes in another, that is what you are hearing.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 id="where">Where the instrument actually radiates</h2>
          <p>
            The viola is not a point source. Low registers radiate broadly from the body,
            while the upper strings become increasingly directional and project up and
            forward from the bridge area. Close-miking one f-hole gets you a bright,
            boxy version of one part of the instrument rather than the whole of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Distance is what mixes the instrument together. Somewhere around a metre is
            where the parts start to blend into what a listener in the room would hear;
            closer than that and you are recording a component, further and you are
            recording the room.
          </p>

          <figure class="fig">
            <div class="ph">
              <span class="ph__label"><strong>Photograph</strong> — the three positions, marked distances<br>to be shot at 2400 × 1600</span>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The same instrument, the same take, three positions. Measured distances to follow.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2 id="positions">Three positions worth trying</h2>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><strong>Above and forward.</strong> Roughly a metre out, raised above the instrument and angled down towards the bridge. The most reliable starting point: it catches the blend without the bow noise sitting on top.</li>
            <li><strong>Level, off the shoulder.</strong> Same distance, microphone at instrument height and slightly to the player's left. Warmer, more body, less bow detail. Useful when the part sits under other material.</li>
            <li><strong>Further back, higher.</strong> Two metres and well above head height. Only works in a room worth recording, but when the room is decent this is the one that sounds like a viola in a space rather than a viola in a microphone.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Record the same eight bars in all three before deciding. Position changes
            the sound far more than any processing you will apply afterwards, and it is
            the one decision that cannot be undone later.
          </p>

          <h2 id="gain">Gain and headroom</h2>
          <p>
            Strings have a wider dynamic range than most sources people record at home,
            and a forte passage can arrive fifteen decibels above where the player was
            warming up. Set the level from the loudest thing in the piece, not from the
            first phrase.
          </p>
          <p>
            At 24-bit there is no reason to push it. Peaks landing around −12 dBFS leave
            room for a surprise without costing anything audible in noise.
          </p>

          <h2 id="player">What the player needs</h2>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>A headphone mix with little or no reverb on their own signal — added reverb encourages under-playing.</li>
            <li>Enough light to read from, and a stand at the right height. Both affect posture, and posture affects tone.</li>
            <li>Fewer, longer takes rather than many short ones. Intonation drifts when a player is stopped every four bars.</li>
            <li>Silence between takes. Talkback that stays open is exhausting to play under.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="after">What to fix afterwards, and what not to</h2>
          <p>
            Worth fixing: a narrow resonance the room added, usually somewhere in the
            low mids and usually audible as one note louder than its neighbours. A
            narrow cut at the offending frequency is a repair.
          </p>
          <p>
            Not worth fixing: a dull recording. Broad top-end lift on a string source
            brings up bow noise and room together, and the result sounds thin rather than
            bright. That one is a placement problem, and the only real fix is to record
            it again.
          </p>

          <div class="pending">
            <strong>Audio comparison outstanding.</strong>
            <p>
              A/B recordings of the three positions, and the measured distances used,
              will be published with this guide once the session audio exists. No
              figures are quoted here that have not been measured.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Reference video. The id goes in data-embed; until one is set the
               frame stays a labelled placeholder rather than a dead player. -->
          <figure class="embed" data-embed="" data-embed-title="Reference recording">
            <div class="embed__frame">
              <div class="embed__prompt">
                <p>Reference video — no source selected yet</p>
                <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="button" data-embed-play disabled>Load video</button>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption class="embed__note">
              External media loads only when you press the button. Until then nothing
              is requested from the platform, no cookie is set and your IP address is
              not transmitted — see the
              <a href="/privacy/" lang="de">Datenschutzerklärung</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>Related</h2>
          <p>
            The samples used in
            <a href="/sound-design/sampled-instruments/building-a-sampled-instrument/">the sampled-instrument build</a>
            came from a session run exactly this way. Whether a part is better played or
            programmed at all is the subject of
            <a href="/recording/">live strings versus sample libraries</a>.
          </p>

          <section class="sources">
            <h2>Sources</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Session notes from the recordings described above. Raw takes retained, not published.</li>
              <li>Speed of sound taken as 343 m/s at 20 °C for the wavelength figures referenced in the tool.</li>
            </ol>
          </section>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <section data-od-id="relaunch-notice">
    <div class="shell relaunch">
      <p>Auspicious Music was relaunched under new ownership in 2026. Historical
         references are preserved for context.</p>
      <a class="link" href="/about/history-and-relaunch/">Read the history and relaunch note
